WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.830
- Hello, I am Pratheek
Karnati, Security Architect

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:04.250
for IBM Cloud.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.000
Today we are going to look
at IBM Cloud Data Shield

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.510
and how it enables you to protect

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:12.310
your containerized applications
data in use or in memory.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.240
We are going to see this
in action with the help

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.320
of a demo application we
built called Cloud Fund.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.270
But first, let's talk
about data protection.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:22.180
We believe it requires protection of data

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.930
throughout its life cycle.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.260
In transit, at rest, and also in use.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.250
And we have been on a
mission to address that

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.750
with confidential computing
which protects data

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.860
in use by performing computation

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.850
in hardware-based trusted
execution environment.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.170
And Data Shield, is a deployment time tool

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.410
that helps you adopt
confidential computing

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.950
on x86 platforms.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.710
Specifically, Intel
Software Guard Extensions

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.680
with no code changes.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.550
Let's see how this works.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.460
Cloud Fund is a Python app
we built for this demo.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.840
And yes, we Data Shield power
by Fortanix Runtime Encryption

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.610
in addition to C/C++.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.060
We also support Java, Python
and RUST runtimes on SGX.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.760
Cloud Fund is a containerized application

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.200
running on Kubernetes,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.540
Cloud Fund is a typical
three-tier application with a UI,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.860
backend API and a database.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.290
For the database, we are using
confidential database service

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.370
from a portfolio called
Hyper Protect MongoDB.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.130
So it is very secure.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.290
For this demo, let's focus
on the threat vectors

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.400
that come at the backend API layer

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.010
and how it privileged
user infrastructure admin,

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.930
for example, with no access
to database can steal

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.240
sensitive data from the
backend API's memory.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.010
As a narrative element for this demo,

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:36.090
we are using Cloud Fund to
donate money to a charity.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.543
Let's see how it works.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.400
So I open the Cloud Fund
application in the browser.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.523
I click donate now.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.220
Here I'm giving the
details and importantly,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.610
I'm giving the personal information

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.210
and also the credit card number.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:58.150
And the expiration date and
the CVV and click submit.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.250
Now the data goes through the backend API.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.350
Now let's see how it privileges
that attack and play out.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.490
Here I'm logged into the
server as privileged user.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.840
So how my terminal open.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.240
I'm trying to log into
the workload in question,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.760
once I'm in there, let's
find the processing question.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.510
The Python app I was talking about.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.750
Once I have that, let's find a PID.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.823
Once I have that let's memory dump it.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.993
Now let's look for strings
in the memory dump.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.753
That's a lot of data
let's ask for my name.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:48.130
As you can see, this is a problem.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.630
This application is vulnerable
to privileges that attacks

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:52.780
targeting its memory contents.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.117
Now let's see how you can
protect your applications data

00:02:55.117 --> 00:02:57.450
and use the Data Shield.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.890
Let's take a look at Data
Shield documentation.

